Sure! The Binary Search Algorithm is a very efficient searching algorithm that works on sorted data. It repeatedly divides in half the portion of the list which contains the item until it finds the item or determines that the item is not in the list.

Here's how you can implement the Binary Search Algorithm in R:

```r
# Function to perform binary search
binary_search <- function(sorted_vector, target) {
  left_index <- 1L
  right_index <- nrow(sorted_vector)

  while (left_index <= right_index) {
    mid_index <- floor((right_index - left_index) / 2) + left_index

    if (sorted_vector[mid_index] == target) {
      return(mid_index)
    } else if (target < sorted_vector[mid_index]) {
      right_index <- mid_index - 1
    } else {
      left_index <- mid_index + 1
    }
  }

  return(-1) # Return -1 if target not found
}

# Example usage:
sorted_vector <- c(2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9)
target_value <- 5

index <- binary_search(sorted_vector, target_value)
if(index >= 0){
  cat(paste("Index:", index))
} else{
  cat("Target Value Not Found")
}
```

To measure the time complexity of the `binary_search` function, we can use the `system.time()` command in R. Here's an example with a sample dataset and target value:

```r
library(microbenchmark)

# Generate a random sorted vector for demonstration purposes
set.seed(123)
random_sorted_vector <- sort(rnorm(100))

# Benchmarking the binary_search function using microbenchmark
bench_result <- microbenchmark(
  binary_search(random_sorted_vector, target_value = median(random_sorted_vector)),
  times = 10
)

print(bench_result)
```
This will give you an idea of how long the binary search process takes. Note that the actual time may vary due to randomness and machine specifics.

The time complexity of the binary search algorithm is O(log(n)), where 'n' is the number of elements in the array. This means that the time required to search doubles approximately every time the size of the input increases by one. Therefore, as the input grows larger, the performance benefits become more pronounced compared to algorithms like linear search which have a worst-case time complexity of O(n).

